---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Panel from '../components/Panel.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Two decades shipping software, and rather less time writing about it. Robotics, .NET, and where AI agents actually fit into a delivery workflow."
>
  <Hero title="About" eyebrow="Who's writing this">
    <p>Why write when you can show? Then agentic dev came along, and the excuses stopped working.</p>
  </Hero>

  <Panel title="At a glance">
    <dl>
      <dt>Based</dt><dd>Ireland</dd>
      <dt>Background</dt><dd>BSc Eng Mechatronics &amp; Robotics, MSc AI</dd>
      <dt>Works on</dt><dd>Backend and platform</dd>
      <dt>Writes about</dt><dd>Engineering, Software, AI Agents</dd>
    </dl>
  </Panel>

  <p>
    I'm an engineer. I started in Mechatronics and Robotics and progressed into an MSc in Artificial Intelligence.
    I've spent a long time building, creating, designing, and shipping things, but significantly less time writing about it.
  </p>
  <p>
    After using the excuse of "too much work", "who's going to maintain it", "social media posts are boring", along came agentic dev and suddenly the excuses no longer hold.
  </p>

  <h2>What I work on</h2>
  <p>
    I've extensive experience in developing and architecting .NET Applications.
    I've been with .NET since 2005, while being relatively competent in C++/C for robotics and IoT applications.
    My day job's focus is distributed .NET Blazor applications.
  </p>
  <p>
    As I've advanced my career I've had a steady interest in the seam between engineering practice, ISO standards (looking at you ISO 27001), and tooling — the parts of delivery that quietly decide whether a team ships monthly or quarterly or not at all because a change breaks all access controls!
  </p>

  <h2>AI Ops</h2>
  <p>
    The other half of what I write about here. Not "AI will write your code", but the more interesting and less glamorous question: where do agents actually fit into an existing SDLC? Review, triage, migration, test scaffolding, documentation that stays current.
    What needs a human gate, what doesn't, and what breaks when you get that boundary wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    I'm sceptical by default and reasonably well-calibrated about it.
    Things that work get written up; so do the things that didn't.
  </p>

  <h2>When I'm not engineering</h2>
  <p>
    When I'm not engineering you can find me exploring latest RPGs on Xbox, PC, or Switch 2 <span role="img" aria-label="game controller">🎮</span>
  </p>
  <p>
    Or planning my next holiday <span role="img" aria-label="plane">✈️</span>
  </p>

  <h2>What you won't find here</h2>
  <p>
    Any employer code. Everything on this site is concepts, patterns and write-ups — generalised deliberately, and written from my own projects where there's code to show.
  </p>

  <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/tim2277">GitHub</a></li>
    <li><a href="/rss.xml">RSS feed</a></li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
